    "body": "Great on any Friday: Anker Nano Car Charger - 75W, One USB-C Port & One Built-In 29\" Retractable USB-C Cable\n1970 Dodge Challenger SE Packs Big-Block Power, Fender Tag Is Missing\n15 photos\nPhoto: Hemmings\nIn 1970, Chrysler debuted the E-body platform. Plymouth used it for the third-generation Barracuda, while Dodge developed the then-new Challenger on it. The latter was the last Mopar to join the muscle car market, and it was quite popular initially, moving nearly 77,000 units in 1970.\nWhile the Barracuda was available in three versions, Dodge only offered two variants of the Challenger. Customers could opt between the standard model and the performance-oriented R/T. However, Dodge also offered a Special Edition (SE) package that turned the Challenger into a more luxurious vehicle (similar to Plymouth's Gran Coupe model).\nThe SE bundle included a vinyl top and, more notably, a smaller rear window. The latter was called the \"formal\" window and had filler panels around it in order to reduce the glass opening. Dodge also added SE medallions on the pillars and extra bright trim on the sides and trunk lid.\nInside the cabin, the SE package added leather and vinyl bucket seats and an overhead console with warning lights or seat belts, low fuel, and door ajar. The woodgrain accents, the smaller-diameter steering wheel, and other details rounded off the cabin's more premium appearance. The package did not nothing performance-wise, as the SE was available with any engine.\nThe bundle was relatively popular, finding its way on 9,976 examples. That's nearly 13% of the total Challenger production in 1970. The package was ordered on 6,223 standard models and 3,753 R/T vehicles. The Hemi SE is the rarest of the bunch, with just 60 units made, while three other versions have three-digit production figures to brag about. I'm talking about the 440 R/T (875 units), the 440 Six-Pack R/T (296 examples), and the standard inline-six (350 units).\nThe yellow hardtop you see here is also a standard model equipped with the SE package, but it was born with a V8 engine. Existing records say that it's one of 5,873 units with a V8 and the SE package, but there's no further breakdown for specific powerplants. The VIN says it was born with a 318-cubic-inch (5.2-liter) small-block V8, which is quite common. It also mates with three-speed automatic, which, again, makes for a very common combo.\nBut I guess it doesn't matter all that much because the Challenger no longer retains the original mill. The 318 was replaced with a 383-cubic-inch (6.3-liter) big-block V8. The latter was available on the Challenger in 1970, but it's unclear if this replacement engine is period-correct . But for what it's worth, the VIN confirms that it's an SE-spec hardtop.\nThe engine swap is not this car's biggest issue, though. The Challenger is also missing its fender tag. This means that we cannot confirm any of the options, and whether the yellow paint is factory-correct or not. The hue appears to be FY1 Top Banana, which was indeed a (brand-new) factory option in 1970.\nIssues aside, this is a pretty solid classic. The Challenger was restored in 1990 and has only covered 8,000 miles (12,875 km) since. The 383 V8 was also rebuilt that year. The seller says it's \"ready to enjoy now,\" which should mean that the car runs and drives as it should. With the same owner for 40 years, the Mopar is looking for a new home for $27,500 . How's the sticker for a non-matching Challenger with a missing fender tag?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nDodge Challenger 1970 dodge challenger classic car pony car muscle car Dodge Mopar\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Car Hunter Bought an Armored Mercedes-Benz S63 AMG, Drove 1,300 Miles and Listed It for Sale",
